Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 06 - Thanksgiving

3
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,840
OK, Philip. This is your third cup of coffee.

4
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:09,880
We should get to work,

5
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,700
or we won't be finished by dinnertime.

6
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:14,920
I guess we must.

7
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,280
We must.

8
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:28,580
OK. The beginning of my famous Thanksgiving apple pie.

9
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:36,200
One apple. Two apples. Three apples. Four apples.

10
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:37,600
Come on, Philip!

11
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,280
Get busy with your famous apple pie.

12
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,760
There's much more to be done.

13
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,800
Now, the ingredients.

14
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:50,900
What goes into my apple pie besides apples?

15
00:02:51,480 --> 00:03:01,100
Ah, yes. Flour, sugar, butter.

16
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,240
Butter, nice and cold and hard.

17
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,120
OK, here are the walnuts.

18
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:15,240
Last but not least,

19
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:19,120
the reason my apple pie is famous - cinnamon.

20
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:20,820
Cinnamon.

21
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:25,480
Ellen, where's the cinnamon?

22
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:27,200
If there is any cinnamon,

23
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:28,900
it's in the cabinet with the salt and pepper.

24
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:45,100
Salt, pepper, dill weed, garlic powder, cinnamon. Ellen?

25
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:46,160
Yes, Philip.

26
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:49,340
Is it possible that we forgot to buy cinnamon?

27
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:56,100
Yes, it is possible that we forgot to buy cinnamon.

28
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:00,160
Well, how can I make my famous apple pie without cinnamon?

29
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:01,780
Good morning.

30
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,180
Oh, hi, Robbie. Good morning.

31
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:05,640
Good morning, Robbie.

32
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:07,940
Can you do me a favor?

33
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:09,120
Sure, Dad. What?

34
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,480
Remember my apple pie on Thanksgiving?

35
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,380
What do you love about it?

36
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:18,480
The apples?

37
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,300
No. The sssss...

38
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:21,720
Cinnamon!

39
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:25,520
Right. We don't have any cinnamon.

40
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,920
I'll go down to Henry's grocery. He's always open.

41
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:29,940
I'll get some for you.

42
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:31,360
That's my boy!

43
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,660
Oh, put your heavy jacket on, Robbie.

44
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:35,620
It's cold outside.

45
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:41,460
Alexandra might call.

46
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:43,400
Tell her I'll call her right back.

47
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,240
OK.

48
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:46,420
Thanks, Son.

49
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:54,140
Uh, why does he always have to slam the door?

50
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:11,060
Hello... Hello, Alexandra. How are you? Fine.

51
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:13,120
Robbie just went to the store.

52
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:14,240
He'll be back soon.

53
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:15,380
He said he'll call you.

54
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:21,300
Oh, oh, I see... Oh... certainly.

55
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:24,240
Well, do you have the phone number there?

56
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:29,360
Oh... I see... Please, I know he wants to talk to you...

57
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:31,380
Thank you,

58
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:34,400
and happy Thanksgiving to you and your family, too.

59
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:38,420
Try to come by later for dessert. Bye.

60
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:42,220
That was Alexandra.

61
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:43,660
She and the Molinas

62
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:45,720
are going to spend Thanksgiving with their cousins.

63
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:47,520
She doesn't have the phone number.

64
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,480
Oh, Robbie will be disappointed.

65
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,920
He'll be grouchy.

66
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,140
Maybe she'll call back. She promised.

67
00:05:58,140 --> 00:05:59,320
Here's your cinnamon, Pop.

68
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:08,000
It was a dollar and sixty cents.

69
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:13,860
You forgot to ask me for the change.

70
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:17,700
Or did you forget to give it to me?

71
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:25,620
Thanks, Son.

72
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:29,520
Alexandra called.

73
00:06:29,620 --> 00:06:30,680
I'll call her back.

74
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:32,300
She said she'll call you later.

75
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:33,600
She's not at home.

76
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:46,240
You should have your breakfast, Son.

77
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:47,320
Make you feel better.

78
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:49,540
Protein, vitamins.

79
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:51,600
She said she'll call back?

80
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:52,640
Yes, she did.

81
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:55,480
Good morning, everyone!

82
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,220
Happy Turkey Day!

83
00:06:59,000 --> 00:06:59,920
What's wrong?

84
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:02,340
Nothing. Absolutely nothing.

85
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:07,200
What's gotten into him?

86
00:07:07,340 --> 00:07:09,140
He missed a phone call.

87
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:10,940
From...?

88
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:12,280
Yes, Alexandra.

89
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:19,160
It's nice to see young love... Oh, to be young again!

90
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:20,640
Where's the coffee?

91
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:39,440
It's possible.

92
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,540
Maybe.

93
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:43,580
It's possible.

94
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:45,460
Could be.

95
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:47,800
It's possible.

96
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,100
We might.

97
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:50,180
We may.

98
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,600
It's possible.

99
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:55,620
Let's see.

100
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:58,240
Is it possible that we forgot to buy cinnamon?

101
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:00,480
It's Thanksgiving Day,

102
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:02,720
and I need to make a pie.

103
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:04,780
We have sugar, apples, walnuts,

104
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:06,780
and time is going by.

105
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:08,760
It's possible there's something

106
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,040
I forgot to buy.

107
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:13,380
I need some cinnamon,

108
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:14,960
or I can't make the pie.

109
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:17,320
Is it possible we forgot?

110
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,180
Maybe we forgot.

111
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:21,360
Is it possible we forgot?

112
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:23,320
Could be.

113
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:25,520
It's possible we forgot.

114
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:27,540
Maybe we forgot.

115
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:30,720
It's possible we forgot.

116
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:33,920
Let's see.

117
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,840
Alexandra might call.

118
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:38,960
It's Thanksgiving Day,

119
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:40,300
and I'm at home.

120
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:42,440
There's someone I want to hear from,

121
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:44,740
and I'm waiting by the phone.

122
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:47,400
She might call me real soon,

123
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:48,700
before we eat.

124
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:51,860
When she does, my Thanksgiving Day

125
00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:52,520
will be complete.

126
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:54,880
Yes, it's possible she'll call.

127
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,820
Maybe she'll call.

128
00:08:56,820 --> 00:08:59,080
It's possible she'll call.

129
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:00,660
Could be.

130
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:03,080
It's possible she'll call.

131
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:05,760
She might, she may.

132
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:08,580
It's possible she'll call.

133
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:10,800
Let's see.

134
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:11,680
Or maybe she'll call back.

135
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:13,180
She promised.

136
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,760
It's possible, maybe.

137
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:17,880
It's possible, could be.

138
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:21,000
It's possible, she might, she may.

139
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:59,020
I love parades.

140
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:03,140
The Thanksgiving Day parade is always such great fun.

141
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:07,140
Look at that Superman balloon!

142
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:13,620
Wowee! Just floating along high above Central Park West.

143
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:15,040
Don't you just love it?

144
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,780
Oh, and the bands and the music.

145
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:20,260
John Philip Sousa.

146
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:21,880
I love his music.

147
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:30,460
Da dada da, da dada da, dada da da la dada da.

148
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:32,580
Oh, look at that float, Robbie.

149
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:35,200
Look at those funny-looking clowns.

150
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:36,820
It's for kids.

151
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:41,500
Maybe so, but parades always make me feel like a kid.

152
00:10:41,900 --> 00:10:45,380
Remember when you and your dad and I

153
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:47,720
went to the Thanksgiving Day parade?

154
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:51,820
You were four or five years old, I think.

155
00:10:57,120 --> 00:10:58,920
Hello, hello...

156
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:02,160
Oh, hi, Susan. How are you?

157
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,420
Here's Mom.

158
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:08,720
Hello, Susan.

159
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:12,040
Yes, he missed a phone call from Alexandra.

160
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:15,100
Yes, I know, but he'll get over it.

161
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:18,240
Good. Then you'll be here about five?

162
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,280
Oh, fine.

163
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:22,720
I look forward to seeing you and Harry and Michelle.

164
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:24,980
Drive carefully.

165
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:26,340
Good-bye.

166
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:29,120
Taste Ellen's turkey dressing.

167
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:30,620
It's delicious.

168
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:32,520
I'm not surprised.

169
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:33,980
It's Grandma's recipe.

170
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:35,700
It's my favorite part of the meal.

171
00:11:35,840 --> 00:11:37,900
What about my famous apple pie?

172
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:41,440
Dad, your apple pie is my favorite dessert.

173
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:42,960
How's the parade?

174
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:43,720
OK.

175
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:56,220
Mom! Is it for me?

176
00:11:56,300 --> 00:11:57,700
No, Robbie.

177
00:11:57,840 --> 00:11:59,040
Who was it?

178
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:01,780
Wrong number.

179
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:04,420
Philip, would you join me in the kitchen, please?

180
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:05,040
It's getting late.

181
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:06,820
We have vegetables to prepare.

182
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:08,020
Can I help with anything?

183
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:08,520
No, honey.

184
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:09,960
You just relax with Grandpa.

185
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:11,920
I'll get you to help serve later.

186
00:12:13,380 --> 00:12:16,140
Ellen reminds me so much of Grandma.

187
00:12:16,220 --> 00:12:18,160
What time does the Michigan football game come on?

188
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:19,000
Four.

189
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:20,020
Philip!

190
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:22,540
I'll be back to see the game.

191
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:28,080
Grandpa, when did Dad graduate from Michigan?

192
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:30,200
Let me think.

193
00:12:30,660 --> 00:12:33,920
He graduated from medical school in 1960

194
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:38,440
and from the University of Michigan in 1956.

195
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:40,560
Did you go to Michigan, too, Grandpa?

196
00:12:40,600 --> 00:12:44,720
Yup. I graduated in 1937.

197
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:47,260
I've got to start thinking about college soon.

198
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:08,800
Grandpa loves the Thanksgiving Day Parade.

199
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:10,920
I love parades.

200
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:16,000
Philip likes to make apple pie.

201
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:16,920
One apple.

202
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:18,840
Two apples.

203
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:22,640
He really enjoys it.

204
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:26,860
And Robbie is fond of Alexandra.

205
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:34,160
Philip likes Ellen's turkey dressing.

206
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:40,820
And Grandpa loves Thanksgiving Day.

207
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,480
Happy Turkey Day!

208
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:46,440
Philip is crazy about football.

209
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:48,680
What time does the Michigan football game come on?

210
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:56,060
And Robbie is crazy about Alexandra.

211
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:05,440
Why does he always have to slam the door?

212
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:08,020
Ellen doesn't like that sound.

213
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:11,720
She can't stand it.

214
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:19,040
And Robbie hates waiting for Alexandra to call.

215
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:24,280
He can't stand waiting.

216
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:34,040
But Robbie really likes his mom's turkey dressing.

217
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:35,560
It's my favorite part of the meal.

218
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:40,720
And you can guess his favorite friend is...

219
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:43,880
That's right, Alexandra.

220
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:46,380
He's fond of her.

221
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:49,200
He's crazy about her.

222
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:52,280
He's wild about her.

223
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:54,700
He likes her a lot.

224
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,400
OK, everybody.

225
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:48,120
I want to welcome Harry and his daughter Michelle

226
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:49,260
to Thanksgiving with us.

227
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:50,820
Thank you, Dr. Stewart.

228
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:51,920
Call me Philip.

229
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:52,360
OK.

230
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:54,940
But first, I think we should take a moment

231
00:15:54,980 --> 00:15:57,580
and remember the meaning of Thanksgiving.

232
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:00,540
Philip, I took Michelle to a school play

233
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:01,700
about the first Thanksgiving.

234
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:04,360
Well, why don't you tell us about that, Michelle?

235
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:06,660
Thanksgiving was about the Pilgrims,

236
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:08,280
the first settlers in America.

237
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:10,660
They shared the first harvest with the Indians

238
00:16:10,700 --> 00:16:11,760
and gave thanks.

239
00:16:11,820 --> 00:16:13,460
All right.

240
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:16,740
Then in that spirit let each of us give thanks.

241
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:18,720
Each in his own way.

242
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:20,060
Who wants to begin?

243
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:21,660
I will.

244
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:27,140
I give thanks for being here with my family and for being well,

245
00:16:27,260 --> 00:16:28,740
so I can enjoy you all.

246
00:16:28,780 --> 00:16:30,100
All right!

247
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:32,140
We love you, Grandpa.

248
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:39,320
I'd like to give thanks for a healthy year, a good job,

249
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:42,000
and for meeting Harry and Michelle.

250
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:45,120
We'd like to give thanks for meeting Susan

251
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:46,480
and the Stewart family.

252
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:48,140
I love you, Daddy.

253
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:49,340
Thanks, Harry.

254
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:50,960
That was very kind of you.

255
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:54,580
I'd like to give thanks for Grandpa coming to live with us.

256
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:56,960
And I'd also like to thank my math teacher

257
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
for giving me a passing grade.

258
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:02,920
And call me, Alexandra.

259
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:04,720
Oh, Robbie!

260
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:06,040
She'll call.

261
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:08,020
You go first, Marilyn.

262
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:09,300
I'm thinking.

263
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:10,560
You go first.

264
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:16,420
Well, you all know I'm working on my photo album.

265
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,340
It's not finished yet.

266
00:17:18,580 --> 00:17:22,280
And I'd like to thank Marilyn for being so patient.

267
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:24,020
Thanks, Richard.

268
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:26,300
I should thank you for encouraging me

269
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:28,500
to keep working on my fashion designs.

270
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:31,700
I'm lucky to have a husband with an artistic eye.

271
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:37,440
Oh, we have a lot to be thankful for.

272
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:40,020
For the food on this table.

273
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:41,440
Just like the Pilgrims.

274
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:43,620
I'll go along with that, Ellen.

275
00:17:44,140 --> 00:17:46,560
Well, help me serve, Robbie.

276
00:17:49,900 --> 00:17:51,560
It was a wonderful meal, Mrs. Stewart.

277
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:52,100
Thank you.

278
00:17:52,220 --> 00:17:54,320
And now to see the end of the football game.

279
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:55,680
Exactly.

280
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:57,520
Where are you going, Philip?

281
00:17:57,840 --> 00:17:59,760
Remember, the Michigan football game?

282
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:02,000
And Michigan needs a touchdown.

283
00:18:02,580 --> 00:18:03,660
Did you forget something?

284
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:06,900
Dad, your famous apple pie.

285
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:08,940
Just let me see the score, Ellen.

286
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:10,360
Go ahead, Philip.

287
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:13,140
We should all take a little break before dessert.

288
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:26,520
Oh, who could that be?

289
00:18:26,540 --> 00:18:29,100
Oh, it must be Alexandra.

290
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:31,440
I invited her to come by for dessert.

291
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:32,700
You did?

292
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:33,900
I like Ellen.

293
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:35,440
You know everyone, Alexandra.

294
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:38,920
No, she doesn't know Harry Bennett and his daughter Michelle.

295
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:39,880
Nice to meet you.

296
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:40,560
Hi.

297
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:42,800
Hello, Alexandra.

298
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:43,540
Hi, Marilyn.

299
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:44,260
Hi, Susan.

300
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:45,420
Happy Thanksgiving.

301
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:48,080
And Alexandra brought us a pumpkin pie.

302
00:18:48,140 --> 00:18:50,200
Please sit down, Alexandra.

303
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:53,240
Dad, Richard, Alexandra's here.

304
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:56,400
Michigan needs a touchdown.

305
00:18:56,420 --> 00:18:57,380
Three minutes to play.

306
00:18:57,480 --> 00:18:58,940
Hi, Alexandra. Welcome.

307
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:00,460
Hello, Alexandra.

308
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:02,180
Yes, Michigan needs a touchdown.

309
00:19:02,300 --> 00:19:05,320
One tiny little touchdown, with just three minutes to play.

310
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:06,940
You want Michigan to win.

311
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:08,240
How'd you guess?

312
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:11,400
And Alexandra brought us a pumpkin pie.

313
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:24,080
What happened?

314
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:26,780
We forgot to turn the oven on.

315
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:29,180
We did?

316
00:19:30,980 --> 00:19:32,960
Philip, why don't you go

317
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:35,000
watch the last three minutes of the game.

318
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:38,080
I will serve coffee and pumpkin pie.

319
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:41,240
OK. I'll be back in a few minutes.

320
00:19:42,040 --> 00:19:44,720
Robbie, would you bring the dessert plates.

321
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:46,360
And, Marilyn, would you pour coffee, please.

322
00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:47,460
Sure, Ellen.

323
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:53,840
How was your Thanksgiving dinner, Alexandra?

324
00:19:53,880 --> 00:19:55,460
Just wonderful, Mr. Stewart.

325
00:19:55,700 --> 00:19:57,760
The Molinas are a large family.

326
00:19:57,820 --> 00:19:59,360
I love being with them.

327
00:19:59,400 --> 00:20:00,880
I'm glad you came by, Alexandra.

328
00:20:00,960 --> 00:20:01,980
I am, too.

329
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:09,040
Touchdown! Touchdown! Touchdown!

330
00:20:11,140 --> 00:20:12,580
Great Thanksgiving.

331
00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:14,920
Lots to be thankful for.

332
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:17,240
Michigan scored a touchdown.

333
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:19,680
Alexandra came by.

334
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:23,360
And nobody misses Philip's famous apple pie.

335
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:25,620
Oh, Grandpa!

336
00:20:34,860 --> 00:20:41,200
On Thanksgiving Day, people in the U.S.A. get together with family and friends.

337
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:46,560
I want to welcome Harry and his daughter, Michelle, to Thanksgiving with us.

338
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:47,560
Thank you, Dr. Stewart.

339
00:20:47,560 --> 00:20:48,400
Hello, Alexandra.

340
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:51,500
Hi, Marilyn. Hi, Susan. Happy Thanksgiving.

341
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:54,360
And Alexandra brought us a pumpkin pie.

342
00:20:54,400 --> 00:20:55,940
Please sit down, Alexandra.

343
00:20:55,940 --> 00:20:57,480
Hi, Alexandra. Welcome.

344
00:20:57,720 --> 00:20:58,920
Hello, Alexandra.

345
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:02,200
The first Thanksgiving was in 1621.

346
00:21:03,160 --> 00:21:06,520
Thanksgiving was about the Pilgrims, the first settlers in America.

347
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,940
They shared the first harvest with the Indians and gave thanks.

348
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:16,800
Thanksgiving is a day with many traditions.

349
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:20,760
People eat traditional foods like turkey

350
00:21:21,140 --> 00:21:22,660
and turkey dressing,

351
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:27,480
and fruit pies like apple pie and pumpkin pie.

352
00:21:29,740 --> 00:21:32,420
Families enjoy modern traditions, too,

353
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:36,040
like watching the Thanksgiving Day parade on television.

354
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:40,600
Don't you just love it?

355
00:21:41,320 --> 00:21:44,560
Oh, and the bands and the music.

356
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:47,560
John Philip Sousa. I love his music.

357
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:51,660
And people watch the afternoon football games.

358
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:54,400
Touchdown! Touchdown!

359
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:58,160
And Thanksgiving is a day for giving thanks.

360
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:01,060
I give thanks for being here with my family

361
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:03,120
and for being well,

362
00:22:03,120 --> 00:22:04,760
so I can enjoy you all.

363
00:22:04,760 --> 00:22:10,280
Well, I'd like to give thanks for a healthy year,

364
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:11,960
a good job,

365
00:22:12,440 --> 00:22:14,680
and for meeting Harry and Michelle.

366
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:17,260
It's a special holiday.

367
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:19,920
A day to be with people you love.

368
00:22:20,280 --> 00:22:21,380
We love you, Grandpa.

369
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:23,340
I love you, Daddy.

